---
// Lesson 1.4a — Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector
// The core build lab: input axis -> direction, direction * speed *
// deltaTime -> vector, transform.Translate. Predict-first, then rebuild
// the starter's movement from a stripped script.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';

const body = `<h1>1.4a — Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Movement is the first real algorithm you build, and it hides the whole
    AP model inside it: <strong>input → processing → output</strong>.
    Today you write the three lines that every game in this course is
    built on.
</p>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Predict First</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Before you type anything, read the shipped
        <code>PlayerController.cs</code> (Assets/Scripts) and find these
        lines in <code>Update()</code>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>xDirection = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal"); // A/D or Left/Right Arrows
yDirection = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");   // W/S or Up/Down Arrows

xVector = new Vector2(xDirection * xSpeed * Time.deltaTime, 0);

transform.Translate(xVector);</code></pre>

    <p>
        Predict, in writing, before you run it: if
        <code>xSpeed</code> is <code>5</code>, how far does the player
        move in one second of holding D? (Careful — the answer is not 5
        pixels.) What does <code>Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")</code>
        return the instant you are not pressing anything?
    </p>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic</h3>
        <p>
            <code>Input.GetAxis("Horizontal")</code> returns 0, or a smooth
            ramp toward +1 (D) / -1 (A). Why is a smooth ramp better for
            movement than <code>Input.GetKeyDown</code>, which is just
            yes/no? What would the game feel like if it snapped between 0
            and 1?
        </p>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~25 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Rebuild it yourself.</strong> Open
        <code>PlayerController.cs</code> and comment out (or delete) every
        line of <code>Update()</code>. Now bring movement back, one piece
        at a time, in this exact order — run the game after each step:
    </p>

    <p><strong>Step 1 — the variables.</strong> You need somewhere for
    input to live. These already exist at the top of the file — find them
    and explain to your neighbor what <code>[SerializeField]</code> is
    doing there (check the Inspector while the game is not running):
    </p>
    <pre><code>[SerializeField] private float xSpeed = 5.0f;
[SerializeField] private float ySpeed = 5.0f;

private float xDirection;
private float yDirection;</code></pre>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — read the input.</strong> Grab the player's intent:
    </p>
    <pre><code>xDirection = Input.GetAxis("Horizontal");
yDirection = Input.GetAxis("Vertical");</code></pre>
    <p>
        Run it. The player still does not move — but the game is already
        <em>reading</em> the player. Nothing happens with data until you
        use it: input is not behavior.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Step 3 — combine into a vector.</strong> Direction alone
    is free; speed alone is nothing. Their product, scaled by how long
    this frame lasted, is <em>motion</em>:
    </p>
    <pre><code>xVector = new Vector2(xDirection * xSpeed * Time.deltaTime, 0);
yVector = new Vector2(0, yDirection * ySpeed * Time.deltaTime);</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Step 4 — move.</strong></p>
    <pre><code>transform.Translate(xVector);
transform.Translate(yVector);</code></pre>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>Why Time.deltaTime?</strong> Update runs once per frame —
        60 times a second on this laptop, 240 on a gaming monitor, maybe
        25 on a friend's potato. Without deltaTime, the player would move
        <em>xSpeed per frame</em>: fast on good machines, slow on bad
        ones. Multiplying by the frame time makes it
        <strong>xSpeed per second, on every machine</strong>. Same input,
        same output, any hardware — that is the abstraction doing its job.
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt: Break the movement on purpose</h3>
        <p>Make each bug, run it, describe what you see, then fix it:</p>
        <ol>
            <li>Change <code>xSpeed</code> to <code>0</code> in the
                Inspector. Which direction still works, and why doesn't
                changing the Inspector break the script?</li>
            <li>Delete just <code>* Time.deltaTime</code> from the
                xVector line. The player now moves about how many times
                faster at 60 fps?</li>
            <li>Swap the arguments: <code>new Vector2(0, xDirection * xSpeed * Time.deltaTime)</code>.
                What did you break and what still works?</li>
            <li>Type <code>Transform.Translate</code> (capital T). What
                does the error mean?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Make it yours</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Give the player a different top speed horizontally than
                vertically (try 8 and 4). What games actually do this,
                and why would a designer want it?</li>
            <li>Set <code>xSpeed</code> to <code>-5</code> in the
                Inspector and play. Describe what happens and why the
                <em>animation</em> still faces the right way.</li>
            <li>Press A and W at the same time. The player moves
                diagonally — is it faster than pure-right? (Measure:
                walk across the screen both ways and count seconds.)
                Games fix this with normalization — file that away for
                the turret unit.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Draw the pipeline: input → direction → × speed → × deltaTime → vector → Translate</li>
            <li>Say what <code>Input.GetAxis</code> returns for A, D, and nothing (−1, +1, 0)</li>
            <li>Explain <code>Time.deltaTime</code> to someone who has never coded</li>
            <li>Explain why the Inspector can override <code>xSpeed</code> without touching the code</li>
            <li>Break and fix a movement bug on purpose in under a minute</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="resources">
    <h3>Reference Docs</h3>
    <p>Need a deeper explanation? These pages cover the same concepts:</p>
    <ul>
        <li><a href="../docs/unit-01/index.html">Unit 1: Setup &amp; Movement</a> — input, vectors, deltaTime in depth</li>
        <li><a href="../docs/reference/unity-csharp-quickref.html">Unity + C# Quick Reference</a> — the API tables</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Coming up next: <a href="1.5a.html">1.5a — Movement II: Speed, deltaTime &amp; Tuning</a></p>
</div>`;
---
<Lesson id="1.4a" title="1.4a Movement I: Input, Direction, Vector — AP CS Principles" root=".." css="../style.css">
  <Fragment set:html={body} />
</Lesson>
